Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^N$ ($N \geq 1$) be a bounded domain with smooth boundary $\partial\Omega$, and let $\mathbf{n}$ denote the outward unit normal on $\partial\Omega$. Fix an exponent $q \in (0,1)$ (strong absorption regime) and a parameter $\lambda > 0$. Consider the semilinear elliptic boundary value problem with strong absorption
$$
\begin{cases}
-\Delta u = \lambda u - u^{q} & \text{in } \Omega, \\
u \geq 0 & \text{in } \Omega, \\
u = 0 & \text{on } \partial\Omega,
\end{cases}
$$
where $u^q$ is extended by $0$ on the set $\{u = 0\}$, and solutions are understood in the weak (or strong $W^{2,p}_{\mathrm{loc}}$) sense.

Suppose that for $\lambda$ in an appropriate range it is known that the problem admits two distinct nontrivial nonnegative solutions:
\begin{itemize}
\item a solution $w_\lambda \geq 0$ such that $w_\lambda = 0$ on $\partial\Omega$ in the usual trace sense;
\item a second solution $u_\lambda \geq 0$ satisfying additionally the overdetermined free-boundary condition
$$\frac{\partial u_\lambda}{\partial \mathbf{n}} = 0 \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega.$$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Question.} Must the second solution $u_\lambda$ be strictly positive throughout $\Omega$, i.e.\ does $u_\lambda(x) > 0$ hold for every $x \in \Omega$?

